MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF CONSUMER AFFAIRS AND PUBLIC DISTRIBUTION ( SHRI SRIRAM CHAUHAN )
(a) Yes, Sir.
(b), (c) & (d) : The Government has been receiving inputs that the current Targeted Public Distribution System (TPDS) suffers from several inadequacies a few of which are as under :
(i) The quantity of foodgrains supplied is insufficient;
(ii) The quality of foodgrains is also sometimes poor;
(iii) Although the objective of TPDS was to focus on poor, the quantities of foodgrains supplied to the population below poverty line is the same as the population above poverty line;
(iv) A better package in favour of poor should be evolved by the Government under T.P.D.S. to make it socially more relevant and economically more attractive;
(v) The subsidy burden under the T.P.D.S. should be reduced by evolving a more positive price policy in favour of poor with more supplies while ensuring adequate supplies to non-poor and ensuring at the same time that the diversion of commodities under the T.P.D.S. is minimized;
(vi) Timely supplies need to be ensured by better organisation of the delivery system;
(vii) Delivery system and issue of ration cards should be transparent and subjected to social audit; and
(viii) Menace of the bogus ration cards should be eleiminated.
Suggested improvements involve the decisions by the Union Government and the State/UT Governments. Review and consequent improvement in the T.P.D.S. is a continuous process and involves the representatives of civil society, academics and experts. Government has decided to evolve a consensus on the issue by associating all concerned in the forseeable future, including the State Government.

